// The sqlite3 binding for sysl -- a package, in the sense `design/packages.md` gives the word.
//
// This is the first sysl package that lives outside the compiler's own repository, so it is also the
// first real reader of this file. There is no `dependencies` block because the binding depends on no
// other sysl package; what it needs is a C library, and `@link("sqlite3")` in the module header is
// where that is said.
//
// This is the block a *consumer* writes to reach this package, and resolution for it is built
// (`design/packages.md` §§ 3-6): a `sysl build` fetches the coordinate, selects by MVS, checks what
// arrived against `sysl.sum`, and compiles it as more modules.
//
//     dependencies {
//       sqlite3 { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/sqlite3", version = "0.3.0" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate is identity rather than a URL, so it carries no `https://`, and the version is the
// tag `v0.3.0` on this repository. Note that the coordinate names the *package*, `sqlite3`, while the
// module a program imports is `sh.sysl.sqlite` -- `packages.md § 9` keeps those two deliberately
// apart. `build-lib` and `--lib` still work, but they are no longer the only way in.

package {
  name    = "sqlite3"
  version = "0.3.0"
}

// SQLite is a hosted library: it opens files, and on this path it reaches the C runtime for them. A
// build for a freestanding target has no operating system to do that, and saying so here is what
// turns "this will not work" into one clean refusal before anything is compiled.
//
// `alloc` is deliberately NOT required. The binding makes strings, so it does allocate -- but the
// requirement a package states is about the *environment*, and a program that cannot allocate will
// be told so at the construction that needs it, in the module that wrote it, which is a better place
// than here.
requires {
  os = true
}
